@inproceedings{WOS:000995182600201,
  abstract = {A positive phrase or a sentence with an underlying negative motive is
usually defined as sarcasm that is widely used in today's social media
platforms such as Facebook, Twitter, Reddit, etc. In recent times active
users in social media platforms are increasing dramatically which raises
the need for an automated NLP-based system that can be utilized in
various tasks such as determining market demand, sentiment analysis,
threat detection, etc. However, since sarcasm usually implies the
opposite meaning and its detection is frequently a challenging issue,
data meaning extraction through an NLP-based model becomes more
complicated. As a result, there has been a lot of study on sarcasm
detection in English over the past several years, and there's been a
noticeable improvement and yet sarcasm detection in the Bangla
language's state remains the same. In this article, we present a
BERT-based system that can achieve 99.60\% while the utilized
traditional machine learning algorithms are only capable of achieving
89.93\%. Additionally, we have employed Local Interpretable
Model-Agnostic Explanations that introduce explainability to our system.
Moreover, we have utilized a newly collected bangla sarcasm dataset,
BanglaSarc that was constructed specifically for the evaluation of this
study. This dataset consists of fresh records of sarcastic and
non-sarcastic comments, the majority of which are acquired from Facebook
and YouTube comment sections.},
