@inproceedings{WOS:001437443700009,
  abstract = {Constructive journalism is a genre that aims to solve the problem of
news avoidance, which can have serious social implications. One major
reason is the negative focus of the news cycle. We investigate if
machine learning models can perform constructive and nonconstructive
classification of news articles. The state-of-the-art BERT language
model was compared with four traditional machine learning methods:
logistic regression, random forest, gradient boosting, and support
vector machine. The traditional models were trained on both metadata and
TF-IDF. Lastly, explainable AI was implemented to gauge whether the
models were trustworthy. The BERT model achieved an accuracy of 81.25\%.
On accuracy, it was outperformed by SVM on metadata (87.50\%) and random
forest on word embedding (89.58\%). However, when using BERT we are able
to make more useful explanations of the model. Due to the fact that it
was able to consider the context of words, whereas the weights of
features are constant in traditional methods.},
