@inproceedings{WOS:001465567400135,
  abstract = {In the field of software engineering, effectively managing software
systems is essential. A key aspect of this management is the
issue-commit link, which connects reported problems or enhancement
requests (issues) with the actual code changes implemented in the
software (commits). However, the robustness of various automated link
recovery techniques, including the leading ML based model Hybrid Linker
remains a subject of discussion. In this study, we investigate the
Hybrid Linker model using interpretability tools like LIME and SHAP to
understand its decision-making, especially its reliance on specific
features. We assess its robustness against adversarial attacks,
revealing its sensitivity to non-textual features like issue and commit
dates. To address this, we introduce ICLNet (Issue Commit Link Network),
which leverages BERT embeddings in a custom neural network. Our
extensive adversarial tests show that ICLNet outperforms Hybrid Linker
in adversarial settings, demonstrating greater resilience. ICLNet
