@inproceedings{WOS:001487072200083,
  abstract = {PET/MRI and MR-guided radiotherapy have significantly advanced clinical
diagnostics and treatment planning by integrating anatomy and function,
while synthetic CT generation from MRI has emerged as a tool for
extracting tissue density and attenuation properties. Despite these
advances, generating synthetic CT from MRI remains challenging in the
pelvic region due to substantial differences in tissue contrast and
intensity between the two modalities. Traditional methods using
dedicated MR sequences often fail to accurately represent bone
structure. Recent deep learning models, including convolutional neural
networks (CNNs) and generative adversarial networks (GANs), have
improved the quality of synthetic CT generation, they typically lack
uncertainty estimation, which limits their clinical utility. In this
study, we propose a conditional diffusion model for generating pelvic CT
from T1-weigted MR, enhanced by tissue segmentation extracted from
TotalSegmentator-MR. We also investigated the self-explanatory
characteristics of the diffusion model through uncertainty mapping from
multiple realizations without requiring ground truth to enhance
interpretability. Our results demonstrate that the diffusion model with
tissue segmentation priors, produces higher-quality synthetic CTs and
improved tissue differentiation compared to deterministic generative
model and diffusion models conditioned only on MR data. Notably, the
uncertainty map showed a strong spatial correlation with the ground
truth-based error map. Furthermore, the inclusion of segmentation priors
also reduces uncertainty, thereby improving the model's reliability.},
