@inproceedings{WOS:001546451000569,
  abstract = {Predicting individualized cognitive developmental outcomes in early
infancy using past (earlier) neuroimaging data has always been a hot
topic. Limited progress has been made due to a lack of methods capturing
highly complex predictive spatiotemporal changes in infant brains.
Instead of using cross-sectional anatomical information, we propose
using existing (i.e., past) longitudinal brain functional connectome
that is considered more informative for individualized developmental
trajectory prediction. Leveraging a pre-trained large language model, we
embody a combination of all the past and the target age time points,
along with the spatial and functional information of all brain regions,
as prior knowledge, to guide multi-task spatiotemporal graph
transformers focusing on age-sensitive developmental connectomic
dynamics, thus facilitating accurate developmental curve prediction. Our
method, coined as Spatiotemporal Transformer with INformation Guidance
(STRING), achieved the best performance on a large dataset compared with
other state-of-the-art methods. We also identified key brain regions
with interpretability of STRING.},
