@inproceedings{WOS:001565104600009,
  abstract = {The initial Transformer architecture which was introduced for text, has
been extended to image, speech and other domains. Multimodal models
which combine more than one kind of data, and vision-language models in
particular, have also seen increasing adoption. The interpretability of
these models is crucial due to their potential for subtle errors and
their diverse applications. Existing interpretability methods for
Transformers primarily employ attention maps to explain vision-language
alignment. This overlooks the contribution from other parts of the
transformer block like Layer Normalization and Feed-Forward Network
(FFN) and can lead to incorrect image and text segment attribution to
the model's decision. We propose an approach that mitigates this issue
by using the output of the transformer modules instead of attention maps
as the basis for deriving the interpretability vectors. We use Spectral
Graph Theory and propose three variants of our method, namely: DSMI
(Deep Spectral Method for Interpretability), DSMI + Grad (DSMI with
gradients) and DSMI + Grad + Attn (DSMI with gradients \& attention
maps). Each version has its own advantages with varying performance
based on the class of models which are being analyzed. We show with
detailed experiments that our methods are superior to some of the
existing interpretability techniques such as GradCAM and have comparable
interpretability to methods like LRP and other state-of-the-art methods
while being simpler to implement.},
