@article{WOS:001597551700001,
  abstract = {Visual intent detection is pivotal in advancing human-computer
interaction, surveillance systems, autonomous vehicles, and assistive
technologies. Practical applications include proactive user interfaces,
driver and operator monitoring, patient and elder-care assistance, and
safety-aware human-robot collaboration. Traditional approaches often
rely on single-modal data, which limits their ability to capture the
multifaceted nature of human intent. This study proposes a novel
ensemble framework that integrates four specialized convolutional neural
networks (CNNs) dedicated to classifying emotion, posture, gesture and
eye gaze direction with a Transformerbased text classifier to accurately
predict human intent. This work also proposes an intent classification
matrix. By leveraging the strengths of CNNs in feature extraction and
Transformers in contextual data processing, this method synthesizes
diverse visual cues into a coherent representation of intent. The
incorporation of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) techniques,
such as Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) and
Captum, enhances the interpretability of the associated models,
providing transparency in the decision-making process. Key findings show
that the cue-specific CNNs and the Transformer achieve strong accuracy
with explicit cue-wise attributions. The Eimage CNN model for emotion
classification reached 71.17 \% training and 68.53 \% testing accuracy
(F1 = 0.18). For posture recognition, the Pimage CNN achieved 79.28
\%/79.14 \% (F1 = 0.23), while the Gestimage CNN attained 96.7 \%/100 \%
(F1 = 1) for gestures. Custom eye gaze direction recognition model
performed at 98.57 \%/89.92 \% (F1 = 0.90), and the Bidirectional
Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) classifier achieved a
perfect 100 \%/100 \% (F1 = 1). This research contributes to the field
by addressing the need for comprehensive, multimodal, visual intent
detection systems that are both accurate and explainable.},
