@article{WOS:001598935800003,
  abstract = {As global supply chains grow increasingly complex and cargo volumes
continue to rise, the accurate prediction of vessel turnaround time
(VTT) has become essential for enhancing port operational efficiency.
Previous studies primarily utilized static features, such as vessel
specifications and container volumes, often failing to capture the
dynamic nature of real-world operations. To address this limitation, we
propose a forecasting framework that incorporates dynamic operation
indicators (OIs) from a two-stage queuing model into time-series models.
Leveraging operational data from Busan Port, our approach achieves of up
to 28\% improvement in mean absolute percentage error compared with
existing techniques. Explainability analysis using Shapley additive
explanations (SHAP) further demonstrates that OIs rank among the most
influential features. By providing more reliable VTT predictions, port
operators can enhance berth planning, optimize resource allocation,
minimize scheduling delays, and enhance overall port efficiency. These
results highlight the practical value of the framework for data-driven
decision-making in port operations.},
